; ======================================================================
;  touch - the Waveshare DSI panel's touch controller and its power MCU
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THIS IS A BOARD FILE AND NOT A CORE ONE, and it is a deliberate
;  choice rather than a shortcut.
;
;  Every other command family in this monitor lives in Anvil/Core and
;  reaches hardware through an Hw* seam, so it compiles on a board that
;  has none of it and degrades to an honest refusal. That is the right
;  shape for a family two boards will ever have.
;
;  This one has exactly one board. There is no HwTouch* vocabulary in
;  hal.pi4 and inventing one for a single implementation would be
;  designing a seam from one example, which is how a seam ends up shaped
;  like its first caller. THE PRESENCE OF THIS FILE IS THE CAPABILITY:
;  board.pi4 includes it, and a board that does not include it does not
;  have the command. When a second board grows a touch panel, the seam
;  gets designed from two examples and this file moves to Core.
;
;  It also has one thing no Hw* seam offers today: I2cWriteRead(), the
;  repeated-start transfer. HwI2cWrite/HwI2cRead cannot express it, and
;  a Goodix read through those two would put a STOP in the middle and
;  return real bytes from the wrong register.
;
;  REQUIRES, and board.pi4 lists them all in order ahead of this file:
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_panel.pi4
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/touch_goodix.pi4
;    Anvil/Core/parse.pbi, format.pi4, rxbreak.pi4 (the usual command kit)
;
;  WHICH BUS - AND THIS CHANGED ON 2026-09-06. i2c.pi4 now drives BOTH
;  BSC masters, so this command talks to the one the panel is actually
;  on: BSC0, GPIO 44/45 at ALT1, the DSI and CSI connectors' bus. No
;  jumper wires. `touch bus header` puts it back on GPIO 2/3 for a panel
;  wired to the header the way the overlay's i2c1 override describes.
;
;  IT PUTS THE HEADER BUS BACK WHEN IT IS DONE. The selected bus is a
;  global inside i2c.pi4 and the core `i2c` command shares it; a `touch`
;  that walked away leaving BSC0 selected would make the next `i2c scan`
;  report BSC0's devices under a heading naming GPIO 2 and 3. HwI2cUp()
;  re-selects the header bus as well, so the two guards cross-check.
;
;  WHICH GENERATION. The bench panel is the v2 board - its overlay line
;  is vc4-kms-dsi-waveshare-panel-v2,10_1_inch_a - so `touch panel`
;  drives Lib/dsi_panel_v2.pi4, the GPIO expander at $94..$99. The v1
;  register file in Lib/dsi_panel.pi4 is NOT driven from here and must
;  not be: $AB and $AD are outside the v2 MCU's regmap and what an
;  out-of-range write does to it is undocumented. The v1 library stays in
;  the tree for a v1 board and is what the desk gate exercises through
;  Examples/Diagnostics/pi4TouchProbe.pi4.
;
;  THE ORDER MATTERS AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. On a v2 board the touch
;  controller's reset line is expander bit 9 - it is not on any Pi GPIO -
;  so the Goodix CANNOT ANSWER until the MCU at $45 has been written.
;  Probing the touch address first therefore reads as a dead bus on a
;  perfectly good panel. DsiV2Begin() runs before Gt9Begin() only when
;  the panel MCU has not already been brought up. Repeating it rewrites
;  the whole expander shadow and can clear live rails and backlight.
; ======================================================================

; How long `touch points` runs before giving up on its own, in polls at
; #TOUCH_POLL_MS each. About thirty seconds. It also stops on a keypress;
; this is the backstop for a session that walked away.
#TOUCH_LIVE_POLLS = 1500
#TOUCH_POLL_MS = 20

; WHICH BUS THE PANEL IS ON is gTouchBus, and it is declared in
; RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_touch.pi4 rather than here. It used to live in
; this file, which was right while this command was the only thing that
; talked to the panel; it is not any more. The boot path, the cursor's
; tick and the payload ABI all reach the same controller now, so one
; copy of "which pair of pins" has to serve all four - and the seam is
; where it belongs. `touch bus display` and `touch bus header` set it.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchUsage - what to type. Printed for a bare `touch` word that is not
;  a subcommand, the same shape i2c_cmd.pi4 uses.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchUsage()
  PrintN("touch talks to the DSI panel's touch controller and its power MCU:")
  PrintN("  touch                        what is on the bus, and what it is")
  PrintN("  touch points                 live coordinates until a key is pressed")
  PrintN("  touch panel power            rails up, touch out of reset")
  PrintN("  touch panel prepare          iovcc, avdd and the panel's own reset")
  PrintN("  touch panel light <n>        backlight, 0 dark to 255 full, DECIMAL")
  PrintN("  touch panel reset            pulse the touch controller's reset")
  PrintN("  touch bus display            use BSC0 on GPIO 44/45 - the default")
  PrintN("  touch bus header             use BSC1 on GPIO 2 and 3 instead")
  PrintN("  touch axes                   the controller-to-panel axis correction")
  PrintN("  touch axes <swap> <invx> <invy>   set it, each 0 or 1, and SAVE it")
  PrintN("The default bus is the DISPLAY connector's, BSC0 on GPIO 44/45 at")
  PrintN("ALT1. No jumpers are needed. The header bus is one command away for")
  PrintN("a panel wired to pins 3 and 5 instead.")
  PrintN("A finger moves the same cursor a USB mouse moves and a tap presses")
  PrintN("the same button, so there is nothing to turn on: touch comes up with")
  PrintN("the panel at boot. `touch axes` is the one thing an eye may have to")
  PrintN("correct, and it is saved, so it is typed once and not again.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchSayBusCode - one #I2C_* code, in words, so a failure names itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchSayBusCode(c.i)
  If c = #I2C_OK
    Print("the transfer succeeded")
  ElseIf c = #I2C_NACK
    Print("nothing acknowledged that address")
  ElseIf c = #I2C_CLKT
    Print("the device held the clock too long")
  ElseIf c = #I2C_TIMEOUT
    Print("the transfer never finished - the bus looks wedged")
  ElseIf c = #I2C_ARG
    Print("the driver refused its own arguments, which is a defect here")
  ElseIf c = #I2C_NOSR
    Print("the repeated start could not be issued, which is a defect in")
    Print(" I2cWriteRead and NOT a missing panel")
  Else
    Print("code ")
    PrintDec(c)
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchSayBus - which controller, which pads, which alt. Printed before
;  anything is probed, because a result nobody can attribute to a pair of
;  wires is not a result.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchSayBus()
  If I2cBus() = #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY
    Print("The bus is BSC0 on GPIO ")
  Else
    Print("The bus is BSC1 on GPIO ")
  EndIf
  PrintDec(I2cBusSda())
  Print(" and ")
  PrintDec(I2cBusScl())
  Print(", at ALT")
  PrintDec(I2cBusAlt())
  Print(", base $")
  PutHexN(I2cBusBase(), 8)
  If I2cBus() = #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY
    PrintN(" - the DSI and CSI connectors.")
  Else
    PrintN(" - the 40-pin header.")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchSayPads - the FSEL code and the pad level of each line.
;
;  THIS IS THE 584 INSTRUMENT. A bus that will not transfer is either
;  unmuxed, or muxed and idle-high with nothing on it, or muxed and held
;  low - and those three want three different answers. Printing the pads
;  BEFORE anything is changed is the only way to know which one arrived.
;
;  On the display connector there are no pull-ups on the Pi: they are on
;  the panel board, through the ribbon. So BOTH LINES LOW HERE IS A CABLE
;  RESULT, not a driver result, and it is the one thing on this bench
;  that can say so without an eye.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchSayPads(when.i)
  If when = 0
    Print("  before the mux: ")
  Else
    Print("  after the mux:  ")
  EndIf
  Print("sda fsel ")
  PrintDec(PinModeGet(I2cBusSda()))
  Print(" level ")
  PrintDec(I2cPadLevel(I2cBusSda()))
  Print(", scl fsel ")
  PrintDec(PinModeGet(I2cBusScl()))
  Print(" level ")
  PrintDec(I2cPadLevel(I2cBusScl()))
  PrintN("")
EndProcedure

; Read-only BSC register evidence. FIFO is intentionally not part of the
; snapshot: reading it would consume a byte and turn a diagnostic into a bus
; operation. These are 32-bit device reads, never byte-assembled `m` output.
Global Dim gTouchI2cSnap.l[7]

Procedure TouchSayController(when.i)
  If I2cSnapshot(@gTouchI2cSnap[0]) = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If when = 0
    PrintN("  controller before probe:")
  Else
    PrintN("  controller after probe:")
  EndIf
  Print("    C $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_C], 8)
  Print("  S $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_S], 8)
  Print("  DLEN $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_DLEN], 8)
  Print("  A $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_A], 8)
  PrintN("")
  Print("    DIV $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_DIV], 8)
  Print("  DEL $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_DEL], 8)
  Print("  CLKT $") : PutHexN(gTouchI2cSnap[#I2C_SNAP_CLKT], 8)
  PrintN("")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchSayMcu - the v2 panel MCU: probe it, read its three identity
;  bytes, power it on. Returns 1 if the MCU answered, else 0.
;
;  IT PROBES BEFORE IT WRITES and it READS BEFORE IT WRITES. An address
;  probe is a zero-length transaction that changes nothing, and the three
;  identity registers are read-only. So the first two things this does
;  cannot alter the state of a panel that is already running, which is
;  what makes `touch` safe to type twice.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i TouchSayMcu()
  Define c.i
  Define ok.i

  c = I2cProbe(#DSI_V2_ADDR)
  If c <> #I2C_OK
    Print("No panel MCU at $45: ")
    TouchSayBusCode(c)
    PrintN(".")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PrintN("A panel control MCU acknowledged $45.")

  If DsiV2Identify() = 0
    Print("  but its identity registers would not read: ")
    TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
    PrintN(".")
    PrintN("  $97, $98 and $99 are the v2 MCU's size, hardware id and firmware")
    PrintN("  version. A part that ACKs and will not read them is either the")
    PrintN("  FIRST-generation MCU, whose register map has none of them, or a")
    PrintN("  bus that can address but not receive. Nothing is written to it")
    PrintN("  from here either way - the v1 registers are outside this MCU's")
    PrintN("  map and what an out-of-range write does is undocumented.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  Print("  size $")
  PutHex2(DsiV2Size())
  Print("  hardware id $")
  PutHex2(DsiV2Id())
  Print("  mcu version $")
  PutHex2(DsiV2Version())
  PrintN("")

  ok = DsiV2Plausible()
  If ok = 0
    PrintN("  ALL THREE ARE $00 or ALL THREE ARE $FF. That is what an undriven")
    PrintN("  or a held bus reads, not what a part answers. Treat the MCU as")
    PrintN("  ABSENT and suspect the ribbon before the driver.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PrintN("  Three bytes came back with content in them, from a part that had")
  PrintN("  to be addressed, hold a register number across a STOP and then")
  PrintN("  drive data. THE RIBBON CARRIES I2C AND IS SEATED. It says nothing")
  PrintN("  about the four differential lanes in the same cable.")
  PrintN("  What the values MEAN is not published anywhere; they are recorded,")
  PrintN("  not interpreted.")

  If DsiV2Ready() = 0
    If DsiV2Begin() = 0
      Print("  the power-on write failed: ")
      TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
      PrintN(".")
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    PrintN("  VCC is on and the touch controller is out of reset (expander")
    PrintN("  $0300). The Goodix could not have answered before this line.")
  Else
    PrintN("  The panel MCU is already up. Its expander shadow was left alone,")
    PrintN("  so this status command did not clear live rails or backlight.")
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchStatus - a bare `touch`. What answered, and what it means.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchStatus()
  Define a.i
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define *idp

  TouchSayBus()
  TouchSayPads(1)
  TouchSayController(0)

  TouchSayMcu()

  ; THROUGH THE SEAM'S OWN BRING-UP, not straight at the driver. It is
  ; the same procedure the boot path and the cursor's lazy attach use,
  ; so a `touch` that revives a controller revives the finger with it -
  ; and there is one place that decides whether the thing is up.
  HwTouchRestart()
  TouchSayController(1)
  a = Gt9Addr()
  If a = 0
    PrintN("No Goodix touch controller answered.")
    Print("  $5D said: ")
    TouchSayBusCode(Gt9CodeHigh())
    PrintN(".")
    Print("  $14 said: ")
    TouchSayBusCode(Gt9CodeLow())
    PrintN(".")
    PrintN("If the MCU above answered and this did not, the touch chip is held")
    PrintN("in reset or is not fitted - NOT a dead bus, because the bus just")
    PrintN("carried three bytes. `touch panel reset` pulses expander bit 9.")
    PrintN("If NEITHER answered, nothing on this bus is alive: check the")
    PrintN("ribbon, and read the pad levels printed above before the driver.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  Print("A Goodix touch controller answered at $")
  PutHex2(a)
  PrintN(".")

  ; THE ID IS PRINTED BYTE BY BYTE, not with Print(*idp). Print's router
  ; picks its formatter from the argument's TYPE, and an untyped pointer
  ; is not a string - Print(*idp) would emit the ADDRESS in decimal with
  ; no diagnostic at all. board.pi4's own header records that exact
  ; defect in PutClockRow, found by a model rather than by a reader.
  *idp = Gt9ProdId()
  If *idp <> 0
    Print("  product id     ")
    i = 0
    While i < 4
      c = PeekA(*idp + i) & $FF
      If c >= 32 And c < 127
        UartWrite(c)
      EndIf
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    PrintN("")
  EndIf
  Print("  firmware       $")
  PutHexN(Gt9Firmware(), 4)
  PrintN("")
  Print("  config version ")
  PrintDec(Gt9ConfigVersion())
  PrintN("")

  ; THE RESOLUTION IS READ OFF THE PART, NOT ASSUMED - and it has to be
  ; printed, because every mapped coordinate is scaled by these two
  ; numbers and a wrong one does not look like an error. It looks like a
  ; finger landing a hand's width from where it touched.
  Print("  reports        ")
  PrintDec(Gt9XMax())
  Print(" by ")
  PrintDec(Gt9YMax())
  Print(", up to ")
  PrintDec(Gt9MaxPoints())
  PrintN(" points at once")
  If Gt9XMax() < 1 Or Gt9YMax() < 1
    PrintN("  A RANGE OF ZERO IS AN UNPROGRAMMED CONFIG BLOCK, not a coordinate.")
    PrintN("  Nothing invents a number in its place, so no touch can be mapped")
    PrintN("  to the screen until the part reports one; the raw values below")
    PrintN("  still read, which is how to tell a dead controller from this.")
  EndIf
  Print("  interrupt      the config block asks for trigger type ")
  PrintDec(Gt9IntTrigger())
  PrintN("")
  PrintN("  and nothing on this board is wired to it, so this driver polls at")
  PrintN("  about 60 Hz - which is what the reference driver does on exactly")
  PrintN("  this wiring. The number is printed, not acted on.")

  ; WHICH PANEL THIS IS, which is worth more than the touch on a first
  ; bring-up: the two generations need completely different display work.
  If a = $14
    PrintN("$14 is the first-generation Waveshare panel: 1280x800 landscape,")
    PrintN("and its display needs NO DSI initialisation sequence at all.")
    PrintN("It is NOT the board this monitor is set up for - `touch panel`")
    PrintN("drives the v2 MCU, whose registers a v1 part does not have.")
  Else
    PrintN("$5D is the DSI-TOUCH generation - the v2 board. Native mode is")
    PrintN("800x1280 PORTRAIT at 60 Hz, 70 MHz pixel clock, two lanes, and")
    PrintN("its display needs a 205-command DCS table before it shows a")
    PrintN("thing. Landscape is a rotation of that, not a mode.")
  EndIf

  TouchSayMapping()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchSayMapping - where a finger lands, and what decides it.
;
;  THE ROTATION IS NOT THIS COMMAND'S TO OWN and it is not repeated
;  here as a setting: it is the SCREEN's rotation, the one `screen
;  rotate` sets and the one the console's own pixels go through. What
;  this prints is which rotation is in force and what it does to a
;  corner, so that a wrong picture and a wrong touch are visibly the
;  same fault rather than two.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TouchSayBit - one bit of the axis code as a 0 or a 1.
;
; A PROCEDURE AND NOT AN EXPRESSION INSIDE PrintDec. Whether a
; comparison yields exactly 1 is a language question this file has no
; business depending on, and a mapping report that printed something
; other than 0 or 1 would be read as a corrupted setting.
Procedure TouchSayBit(f.i, bit.i)
  If (f & bit) <> 0
    Print("1")
  Else
    Print("0")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure TouchSayMapping()
  Define f.i
  Define st.i
  Define deg.i
  Define lw.i
  Define lh.i
  Define xm.i
  Define ym.i

  f = HwTouchFlip()
  st = TouchFlipStored()
  deg = TouchRotation()
  lw = TouchSurfaceW()
  lh = TouchSurfaceH()
  xm = Gt9XMax()
  ym = Gt9YMax()

  Print("The controller's axes are taken as ")
  If f = #SCR_TOUCH_ALIGNED
    Print("ALIGNED with the panel's")
  Else
    Print("corrected: swap ")
    TouchSayBit(f, #SCR_TOUCH_SWAP)
    Print(", invert-x ")
    TouchSayBit(f, #SCR_TOUCH_INVX)
    Print(", invert-y ")
    TouchSayBit(f, #SCR_TOUCH_INVY)
  EndIf
  Print(" (code ")
  PrintDec(f)
  PrintN(").")

  If ScrTouchFlipOk(st) = 0
    PrintN("SETTINGS.TXT names no axis correction, so that is the DEFAULT and")
    PrintN("not something stored - which is what the overlay says about this")
    PrintN("panel: the 10.1 inch a variant sets no swap and no inversion.")
  Else
    Print("SETTINGS.TXT holds ")
    PrintDec(st)
    PrintN(", so this is a stored answer and survives a reset.")
  EndIf

  Print("The console on the panel is turned ")
  PrintDec(deg)
  Print(" degrees and is ")
  PrintDec(lw)
  Print(" by ")
  PrintDec(lh)
  PrintN(" pixels;")
  PrintN("a touch is scaled onto exactly that surface, so a change of screen")
  PrintN("rotation or of scale moves the finger with the picture and needs")
  PrintN("nothing typed here.")
  If gScrSrc <> #SCR_SRC_DSI
    PrintN("THE CONSOLE IS NOT ON THE PANEL AT THE MOMENT, so those are the")
    PrintN("numbers the panel WOULD come up at. A finger cannot move a cursor")
    PrintN("on a screen it is not on, so nothing is moved until `screen on dsi`.")
  EndIf

  If xm > 0 And ym > 0
    PrintN("Where the corners land, computed rather than promised:")
    Print("  controller 0,0            -> screen ")
    PrintDec(ScrTouchX(deg, f, 0, 0, xm, ym, lw, lh))
    Print(",")
    PrintDec(ScrTouchY(deg, f, 0, 0, xm, ym, lw, lh))
    PrintN("")
    Print("  controller ")
    PrintDec(xm - 1)
    Print(",")
    PrintDec(ym - 1)
    Print("  -> screen ")
    PrintDec(ScrTouchX(deg, f, xm - 1, ym - 1, xm, ym, lw, lh))
    Print(",")
    PrintDec(ScrTouchY(deg, f, xm - 1, ym - 1, xm, ym, lw, lh))
    PrintN("")
  EndIf
  PrintN("`touch points` and one drag corner to corner is how the mapping is")
  PrintN("checked against a finger; `touch axes` is how it changes, and it is")
  PrintN("saved, so it is typed once.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchLive - `touch points`. Every contact, raw and mapped, until a
;  key is pressed.
;
;  IT RUNS THROUGH THE SEAM, so what it prints is exactly what a UI and
;  the cursor are being given - the same read, the same mapping, the
;  same frame. A separate polling loop of its own would be a second
;  opinion, and the whole point of typing this command is to find out
;  whether the first one is right.
;
;  THE CURSOR MOVES WHILE IT RUNS, for the same reason: HwTouchTick is
;  the one reader, so a finger dragged during this command is a finger
;  dragging the arrow on the glass, and the numbers on the wire and the
;  picture on the panel are the same event seen twice.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchLive()
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define polls.i
  Define seen.i
  Define last.i

  If HwTouchReady() = 0
    If HwTouchRestart() = 0
      PrintN("!! no touch controller answered. Run `touch` for the reason.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  EndIf

  PrintN("Reporting every frame that changes. Press any key to stop.")
  PrintN("raw is what the controller said; screen is where that lands on the")
  PrintN("console, through the same rotation and scale the picture uses.")
  polls = 0
  seen = 0
  last = -1
  While polls < #TOUCH_LIVE_POLLS
    If OutBreak() <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
    n = HwTouchTick()
    If n < 0
      Print("!! a transfer failed: ")
      TouchSayBusCode(Gt9LastBusCode())
      PrintN(".")
      Break
    EndIf
    ; ONLY WHEN SOMETHING IS DOWN, AND ONLY WHEN IT CHANGED. A finger
    ; resting still produces a frame every 17 ms; printing all of them
    ; would scroll the very console the finger is being tested against
    ; and, at 90 degrees, spend a full-screen transpose on each one.
    If n > 0
      If HwTouchQueued() > 0 Or n <> last
        seen = seen + 1
        i = 0
        While i < n
          Print("  id ")
          PrintDec(HwTouchPointId(i))
          Print("  raw ")
          PrintDec(HwTouchPointRawX(i))
          Print(",")
          PrintDec(HwTouchPointRawY(i))
          Print("  screen ")
          PrintDec(HwTouchPointX(i))
          Print(",")
          PrintDec(HwTouchPointY(i))
          Print("  size ")
          PrintDec(HwTouchPointSize(i))
          PrintN("")
          i = i + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
    EndIf
    last = n
    ; AND THE SAME DRAIN THE PROMPT DOES, so the arrow follows the
    ; finger while this command holds the console. Two reasons and both
    ; matter: the ring would otherwise fill and start dropping the
    ; OLDEST event, which is the DOWN; and a report that printed
    ; coordinates while the cursor stood still would be testing half the
    ; path. TouchTick's own read is rate limited, so this costs nothing.
    TouchTick()
    delay(#TOUCH_POLL_MS)
    polls = polls + 1
  Wend

  Print("Stopped after ")
  PrintDec(polls)
  Print(" polls; ")
  PrintDec(seen)
  Print(" report(s) printed, ")
  PrintDec(Gt9Frames())
  Print(" frames arrived, ")
  PrintDec(HwTouchDowns())
  Print(" down, ")
  PrintDec(HwTouchMoves())
  Print(" move, ")
  PrintDec(HwTouchUps())
  PrintN(" up.")
  If Gt9Frames() = 1
    PrintN("EXACTLY ONE frame and then nothing is the signature of the")
    PrintN("buffer flag never being released. That would be a defect in")
    PrintN("Gt9Poll, not a panel that stopped being touched.")
  EndIf
  If HwTouchLost() > 0
    Print("!! ")
    PrintDec(HwTouchLost())
    PrintN(" event(s) have been dropped for a full queue since boot. That is a")
    PrintN("   queue nobody drained, not a controller fault.")
  EndIf
  If HwTouchUnmapped() > 0
    Print("!! ")
    PrintDec(HwTouchUnmapped())
    PrintN(" contact(s) could not be mapped to the screen since boot - the")
    PrintN("   controller would not say what its coordinate range is, so there")
    PrintN("   is no number to scale by. `touch` prints the range it reported.")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchPanel - `touch panel ...`. gPos is past the word "panel".
;
;  Four verbs, and they are deliberately the four STEPS rather than one
;  "bring the panel up": every one of them is a separate thing that can
;  fail, and a single command that did all four would report the last
;  failure and hide the first. On a bring-up bench the ladder IS the
;  instrument.
;
;    power    the MCU's own probe write: VCC on, touch reset released
;    prepare  the panel's rails and its reset, exactly as the panel
;             driver does them before it sends one DCS byte
;    light    the backlight - and this is the stage a human eye grades
;    reset    pulse the touch controller's reset, expander bit 9
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchPanel()
  Define v.i

  SkipSpace()
  If gLine[gPos] = 0
    PrintN("!! touch panel needs power, prepare, light <n> or reset.")
    PrintN("   Nothing was done.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  gWordAt = gPos
  SkipWord()
  gWordLen = gPos - gWordAt

  If WordIs("power") <> 0
    If DsiV2Ready() <> 0
      PrintN("The panel MCU is already up. Nothing was rewritten: repeating the")
      PrintN("power-on write could clear live rails and backlight.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    If DsiV2Begin() = 0
      Print("!! the power-on write failed: ")
      TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
      PrintN(". Nothing is powered.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    Print("Expander written to $")
    PutHexN(DsiV2Pins(), 4)
    PrintN(": VCC on, touch controller out of reset.")
    PrintN("That is what the Linux probe does and no more. The panel's own")
    PrintN("rails are `touch panel prepare`; the light is `touch panel light`.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  If WordIs("prepare") <> 0
    If DsiV2Ready() = 0
      PrintN("!! run `touch panel power` first. Nothing was done.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    If DsiV2Prepare() = 0
      Print("!! the prepare sequence failed: ")
      TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
      PrintN(".")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    Print("iovcc, avdd, then reset low 60 ms and high 60 ms. Expander $")
    PutHexN(DsiV2Pins(), 4)
    PrintN(".")
    PrintN("THE PANEL IS POWERED AND STILL BLANK, and that is correct: it")
    PrintN("shows nothing until the DCS init table has gone over DSI and")
    PrintN("video is running. A lit backlight over a blank panel is a RESULT.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  If WordIs("reset") <> 0
    If DsiV2Ready() = 0
      PrintN("!! run `touch panel power` first. Nothing was done.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    If DsiV2TouchReset(20) = 0
      Print("!! the reset pulse failed: ")
      TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
      PrintN(".")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    PrintN("Expander bit 9 pulsed low for 20 ms. Nothing on this board reaches")
    PrintN("the Goodix reset except that bit, so this is the only reset there")
    PrintN("is. Run `touch` again to see whether it answers now.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  If WordIs("light") <> 0
    ; DECIMAL, like clock and baud and speed, and unlike every address in
    ; this monitor. Said in the usage text because a brightness typed as
    ; hex is a working screen at the wrong level, which nobody notices.
    v = ParseDec()
    If gParseOk = 0
      PrintN("!! touch panel light needs a DECIMAL level, 0 to 255.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    If DsiV2Ready() = 0
      If DsiV2Begin() = 0
        Print("!! no panel MCU at $45: ")
        TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
        PrintN(". Nothing was done.")
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
    EndIf
    If DsiV2Backlight(v) = 0
      If v < 0 Or v > 255
        PrintN("!! the level must be 0 to 255. Nothing was done.")
      Else
        Print("!! the backlight write failed: ")
        TouchSayBusCode(DsiV2BusCode())
        PrintN(".")
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    Print("Backlight enable ")
    If v = 0
      Print("off")
    Else
      Print("on")
    EndIf
    Print(", PWM register $96 set to ")
    PrintDec(v)
    PrintN(" of 255.")
    PrintN("THIS MCU IS NOT INVERTED - the first-generation one takes $FF")
    PrintN("minus the level and this one takes the level. If the light does")
    PrintN("not change, that is a wiring or a generation answer, not a")
    PrintN("missing commit write: this generation has no commit register.")
    PrintN("A LADDER, NOT ONE VALUE: try 0, then 128, then 255. One write")
    PrintN("cannot tell a working backlight from a stuck one.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  PrintN("!! touch panel does not know that word. Nothing was done.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchBus - `touch bus display` / `touch bus header`.
;
;  It does NOT bring the new bus up: selecting is not configuring, and
;  the next `touch` line does that. Printing what changed without
;  changing four pads is the whole point - see I2cSelectBus.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchBus()
  SkipSpace()
  If gLine[gPos] = 0
    ; ***SELECT FIRST, THEN REPORT, AND RESTORE.*** TouchSayBus() prints
    ; whatever bus i2c.pi4 currently has selected, and between commands
    ; that is always the header bus, because every touch command puts it
    ; back. Measured on silicon 2026-09-06: a bare `touch bus` answered
    ; "BSC1 on GPIO 2 and 3" while the very next `touch` used BSC0. It
    ; was a true sentence about the wrong question - the operator is
    ; asking which bus THIS COMMAND uses, not which one the library is
    ; parked on - and a true sentence about the wrong question is the
    ; kind of thing that gets written down.
    I2cSelectBus(gTouchBus)
    TouchSayBus()
    I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
    PrintN("Type `touch bus display` or `touch bus header` to change it.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  gWordAt = gPos
  SkipWord()
  gWordLen = gPos - gWordAt

  If WordIs("display") <> 0
    gTouchBus = #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY
  ElseIf WordIs("header") <> 0
    gTouchBus = #I2C_BUS_HEADER
  Else
    PrintN("!! touch bus takes display or header. Nothing was changed.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  I2cSelectBus(gTouchBus)
  TouchSayBus()
  TouchSayPads(0)
  PrintN("Nothing was muxed and nothing was enabled - the next touch command")
  PrintN("does that. The levels above are the pads as the firmware left them.")
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TouchAxes - `touch axes` reports, `touch axes <swap> <invx> <invy>`
;  sets AND SAVES.
;
;  ***IT IS SAVED, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF IT.*** Which way the touch
;  controller counts against the panel it is glued to is a fact about
;  the panel, like which way up the panel is bolted - and `screen
;  rotate` is stored for exactly that reason. A correction that had to
;  be retyped after every reset would mean the board did NOT come up
;  with working touch, which is the requirement.
;
;  It is not the rotation and it does not duplicate it. The rotation is
;  the screen's and is set with `screen rotate`; this is only the
;  controller's axes against the panel's, which the device tree states
;  per panel model and states as "nothing" for the panel on this bench.
;  Three numbers in, one code stored - see #SCR_TOUCH_SWAP.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TouchAxes()
  Define sw.i
  Define ix.i
  Define iy.i
  Define f.i
  Dim buf.a[16]

  SkipSpace()
  If gLine[gPos] = 0
    TouchSayMapping()
    PrintN("Type `touch axes <swap> <invert-x> <invert-y>`, each 0 or 1, to")
    PrintN("change it. Try one thing at a time: if a finger moves the cursor")
    PrintN("the right way across but the wrong way down, that is invert-y")
    PrintN("alone, and swapping as well would move it sideways instead.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  sw = ParseDec()
  If gParseOk = 0
    PrintN("!! touch axes needs three numbers: swap, invert-x, invert-y.")
    PrintN("   Each is 0 or 1, decimal. Nothing was done.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  ix = ParseDec()
  If gParseOk = 0
    PrintN("!! touch axes needs three numbers: swap, invert-x, invert-y.")
    PrintN("   Each is 0 or 1, decimal. Nothing was done.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  iy = ParseDec()
  If gParseOk = 0
    PrintN("!! touch axes needs three numbers: swap, invert-x, invert-y.")
    PrintN("   Each is 0 or 1, decimal. Nothing was done.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  If sw < 0 Or sw > 1 Or ix < 0 Or ix > 1 Or iy < 0 Or iy > 1
    PrintN("!! each of the three is 0 or 1 and nothing else, because each one")
    PrintN("   is a yes or a no about an axis. Nothing was changed.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  f = 0
  If sw <> 0
    f = f | #SCR_TOUCH_SWAP
  EndIf
  If ix <> 0
    f = f | #SCR_TOUCH_INVX
  EndIf
  If iy <> 0
    f = f | #SCR_TOUCH_INVY
  EndIf

  ; STORE FIRST, THEN APPLY - the same order `screen rotate` uses, and
  ; for the same reason: a correction that is on the glass but not in
  ; the store is one that vanishes at the next reset without saying so,
  ; and the operator who typed it has no way to tell.
  PmfPutDec(f, @buf[0])
  If SettingsSet(TouchAxesKey(), @buf[0]) = 0
    PrintN("!! the axis correction could not be recorded in the settings store,")
    PrintN("   so it was not applied either. Nothing was changed.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If SettingsSave() = 0
    PrintN("!! the settings store would not write to the boot medium, so this")
    PrintN("   correction is in memory only and will be gone at the next reset.")
    PrintN("   It is being applied anyway; type settings save when the medium")
    PrintN("   is back to make it stick.")
  EndIf

  If HwTouchSetFlip(f) = 0
    PrintN("!! the driver refused that combination, which is a defect here and")
    PrintN("   not a typing mistake - the store now holds it and the driver")
    PrintN("   does not. Report it.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  TouchSayMapping()
  PrintN("It is saved, so the panel comes up mapped this way with nothing")
  PrintN("typed. Drag one finger corner to corner and check `touch points`.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CmdTouch - the dispatcher. gPos is already past "touch".
;
;  THE BUS COMES UP HERE AND ONLY HERE, the same shape CmdI2c uses: the
;  subcommand is decided before any hardware is touched, so an unknown
;  word cannot leave the bus half-configured.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure CmdTouch()
  Define which.i
  Define up.i

  SkipSpace()
  If gLine[gPos] = 0
    ; A bare `touch` is the status report, not the usage text - status is
    ; what somebody typing one word wants, and the usage is one line away
    ; from any wrong word.
    which = 1
  Else
    gWordAt = gPos
    SkipWord()
    gWordLen = gPos - gWordAt

    which = 0
    If WordIs("points") <> 0
      which = 2
    ElseIf WordIs("panel") <> 0
      which = 3
    ElseIf WordIs("axes") <> 0
      which = 4
    ElseIf WordIs("help") <> 0
      which = 5
    ElseIf WordIs("bus") <> 0
      which = 6
    EndIf

    If which = 0
      PrintN("!! touch does not know that subcommand.")
      TouchUsage()
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  EndIf

  If which = 5
    TouchUsage()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; `touch bus` reports and selects without configuring anything, so it
  ; runs OUTSIDE the bring-up below - that is what makes it able to print
  ; the pads as the firmware left them.
  If which = 6
    TouchBus()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; `touch axes` is arithmetic, not a transfer. Bringing a bus up for it
  ; would mux two pads to answer a question about a coordinate mapping.
  If which = 4
    TouchAxes()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ---- THE BUS, AND THE PADS BEFORE IT IS TOUCHED --------------------
  ; The reading BEFORE the mux is the one that cannot be taken later, so
  ; it is taken here, on the path every hardware subcommand goes through.
  I2cSelectBus(gTouchBus)
  If gTouchBus = #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY
    TouchSayPads(0)
  EndIf

  up = I2cUp()

  ; ---- AND THE HEADER BUS GOES BACK, WHATEVER HAPPENS ----------------
  ; Every return path below is inside this procedure so the restore at
  ; the end is unavoidable. A `touch` that left BSC0 selected would make
  ; the next `i2c scan` describe BSC0's devices under a heading naming
  ; GPIO 2 and 3.
  If up = 0
    PrintN("!! the I2C bus would not come up. Nothing was done.")
  ElseIf which = 1
    TouchStatus()
  ElseIf which = 2
    TouchLive()
  Else
    TouchPanel()
  EndIf

  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
EndProcedure
